WEBVTT

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:02.470
What changes when the circle grows?

00:00:02.470 --> 00:00:08.315
On the real interval, the usual Legendre polynomials stayed within one in absolute value.

00:00:08.315 --> 00:00:11.131
Keep the polynomial and move the circle outward.

00:00:11.431 --> 00:00:13.300
Begin with a point on a circle.

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:16.286
Its radius is rho and its angle is theta.

00:00:16.286 --> 00:00:17.608
Fix rho at two.

00:00:17.608 --> 00:00:20.744
As theta changes, z travels around the circle.

00:00:21.044 --> 00:00:23.276
The reciprocal runs the other way.

00:00:23.276 --> 00:00:27.009
Its radius is one half, and its angle is minus theta.

00:00:27.009 --> 00:00:29.803
Join the two moving points and mark their midpoint.

00:00:30.103 --> 00:00:31.930
The midpoint traces an ellipse.

00:00:31.930 --> 00:00:35.172
The Joukowski map sends z to this midpoint, omega.

00:00:35.172 --> 00:00:38.372
Here on the ellipse is where we evaluate the polynomial.

00:00:38.672 --> 00:00:40.349
Read the two semiaxes.

00:00:40.349 --> 00:00:44.721
At angle zero, the midpoint is five quarters to the right of the origin.

00:00:44.721 --> 00:00:49.499
After a quarter turn, the two points lie on opposite sides of the real axis.

00:00:49.499 --> 00:00:51.675
Their midpoint is three quarters above it.

00:00:51.975 --> 00:00:54.292
The interval is the limiting picture.

00:00:54.292 --> 00:00:59.049
At radius one the vertical semiaxis vanishes, leaving the real interval.

00:00:59.049 --> 00:01:02.313
Increase rho beyond one and the ellipse opens up again.

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:04.937
Now follow the output.

00:01:04.937 --> 00:01:07.027
The point on the ellipse is our input.

00:01:07.027 --> 00:01:11.998
Add the weighted powers of z in a second plane to find the polynomial value.

00:01:12.298 --> 00:01:14.017
Each power has a frequency.

00:01:14.017 --> 00:01:16.833
Term j has frequency n minus twice j.

00:01:16.833 --> 00:01:21.313
At degree three the frequencies are three, one, minus one and minus three.

00:01:21.613 --> 00:01:23.418
Radius sets the arrow length.

00:01:23.418 --> 00:01:26.297
The length is the weight times rho to the power m.

00:01:26.297 --> 00:01:32.932
Positive powers grow as rho increases, negative powers shrink, and the zero frequency keeps its weight.

00:01:33.232 --> 00:01:34.866
Angle sets the direction.

00:01:34.866 --> 00:01:37.127
An arrow turns through m times theta.

00:01:37.127 --> 00:01:41.585
Positive frequencies turn anticlockwise, negative ones clockwise.

00:01:41.585 --> 00:01:43.377
A zero frequency stays still.

00:01:43.677 --> 00:01:45.290
Start with the cubic arrow.

00:01:45.290 --> 00:01:49.001
Five sixteenths times two cubed gives a length of five halves.

00:01:49.001 --> 00:01:51.903
Three times thirty degrees points the arrow straight up.

00:01:52.203 --> 00:01:54.285
Place the middle arrows head to tail.

00:01:54.285 --> 00:01:58.871
With weight three sixteenths, their lengths are three eighths and three over thirty-two.

00:01:58.871 --> 00:02:03.266
One points thirty degrees above horizontal, the other thirty degrees below.

00:02:03.566 --> 00:02:05.264
Magnify the last arrow.

00:02:05.264 --> 00:02:08.272
Follow the camera into the cluster at the end of the chain.

00:02:08.272 --> 00:02:10.234
The pink arrow points straight down.

00:02:10.234 --> 00:02:16.142
The reciprocal cube contributes one eighth, making the true length five over one hundred twenty-eight.

00:02:16.142 --> 00:02:19.939
At thirty-two times magnification, its tail and tip separate.

00:02:19.939 --> 00:02:21.475
Pull back to the whole chain.

00:02:21.775 --> 00:02:24.007
Join the origin to the final tip.

00:02:24.007 --> 00:02:27.782
The gold arrow gives P three of omega in the output plane.

00:02:27.782 --> 00:02:30.299
Its input, omega, stays on the ellipse.

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:33.150
How long can the sum become?

00:02:33.150 --> 00:02:36.904
Follow the same angle theta through all four boundary waves.

00:02:36.904 --> 00:02:42.749
At the moving marker, take each mode with its full complex direction and its own radial length.

00:02:42.749 --> 00:02:45.287
Now place those four arrows head to tail.

00:02:45.287 --> 00:02:49.105
Their gold resultant runs from the origin to the last tip.

00:02:49.105 --> 00:02:53.223
At thirty degrees the path turns corners, so the direct route is shorter.

00:02:53.523 --> 00:02:54.880
Add the four lengths.

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:57.162
These are lengths of individual arrows.

00:02:57.162 --> 00:03:00.169
Add them along one straight ruler to get capital S.

00:03:00.169 --> 00:03:01.683
Turn the shared angle.

00:03:01.683 --> 00:03:07.037
The four phases move, the chain changes shape, and its tip moves inside the circle.

00:03:07.037 --> 00:03:08.786
Its path length stays fixed.

00:03:08.786 --> 00:03:13.223
The triangle inequality gives a disk of radius S for every angle.

00:03:13.523 --> 00:03:15.648
Alignment attains the supremum.

00:03:15.648 --> 00:03:19.872
At angle zero, all arrows point right and their sum reaches S.

00:03:19.872 --> 00:03:27.317
The polynomial is analytic, so the maximum modulus principle gives the same supremum over the ellipse interior.

00:03:27.617 --> 00:03:29.465
Pair the opposite frequencies.

00:03:29.465 --> 00:03:32.600
The terms at m and minus m have equal weights.

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:35.565
Write lowercase s for the logarithm of rho.

00:03:35.565 --> 00:03:38.594
The radial factors now have opposite exponents.

00:03:38.894 --> 00:03:40.571
Trace the boundary waves.

00:03:40.571 --> 00:03:42.960
Theta marks position around the ellipse.

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:47.823
Draw the real part of each angular mode at a common height to compare its shape.

00:03:47.823 --> 00:03:51.364
The modes plus one and minus one each have one crest.

00:03:51.364 --> 00:03:54.350
Plus three and minus three each have three crests.

00:03:54.350 --> 00:03:58.083
Advance the same display phase, phi, for every wave.

00:03:58.083 --> 00:04:01.283
Opposite signs send the crests in opposite directions.

00:04:01.583 --> 00:04:04.198
Degree five adds two more frequencies.

00:04:04.198 --> 00:04:08.848
Degree five keeps the frequencies plus and minus one and three, with new weights.

00:04:08.848 --> 00:04:11.962
Its extra powers are plus five and minus five.

00:04:11.962 --> 00:04:14.351
Each new wave has five crests per circuit.

00:04:14.351 --> 00:04:19.044
The exponent tells us both the radial power and the signed angular frequency.

00:04:19.044 --> 00:04:22.009
The number of crests counts cycles along the boundary.

00:04:22.309 --> 00:04:23.922
Pairing preserves the total.

00:04:23.922 --> 00:04:27.057
Watch the two cubic waves while we compare their lengths.

00:04:27.057 --> 00:04:30.747
At radius two, their radial factors are eight and one eighth.

00:04:30.747 --> 00:04:32.795
Both carry weight five sixteenths.

00:04:32.795 --> 00:04:38.597
The long arrow and the short arrow contribute five halves and five over one hundred twenty-eight.

00:04:38.597 --> 00:04:40.900
Slide those lengths together on the ruler.

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:43.929
Now divide the same bar into two equal shares.

00:04:43.929 --> 00:04:48.110
Its total is still three hundred twenty-five over one hundred twenty-eight.

00:04:48.110 --> 00:04:50.541
The waves keep their actual amplitudes.

00:04:50.541 --> 00:04:53.100
Only the way we record the pair has changed.

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:59.863
For any opposite pair, the average of the two radial factors is the hyperbolic cosine of m times s.

00:05:00.163 --> 00:05:02.544
The largest frequency gives a ceiling.

00:05:02.544 --> 00:05:08.069
Overlay the one-crest and three-crest profiles to see which frequencies the cubic contains.

00:05:08.069 --> 00:05:10.970
The estimate compares their paired radial factors.

00:05:10.970 --> 00:05:15.129
At radius two, the factor for magnitude one is five quarters.

00:05:15.129 --> 00:05:18.350
For magnitude three it is sixty-five sixteenths.

00:05:18.350 --> 00:05:19.906
Follow the four rows.

00:05:19.906 --> 00:05:23.490
The outer rows already have frequency magnitude three.

00:05:23.490 --> 00:05:28.076
Raise each inner row from its own factor to the larger one, keeping its weight.

00:05:28.076 --> 00:05:33.174
Hyperbolic cosine is even and increases with the magnitude of its argument.

00:05:33.174 --> 00:05:39.339
The weights sum to one, so this gives the ceiling B, the average of rho to the n and rho to the minus n.

00:05:39.639 --> 00:05:41.124
There are two different gaps.

00:05:41.124 --> 00:05:45.796
At thirty degrees, the gold resultant has length about two point six three three.

00:05:45.796 --> 00:05:49.678
Compare it with the path length, about three point zero zero eight.

00:05:49.678 --> 00:05:50.978
Turn to zero.

00:05:50.978 --> 00:05:53.836
The arrows align and that first gap closes.

00:05:53.836 --> 00:05:55.521
The second gap stays put.

00:05:55.521 --> 00:06:05.654
Replacing the inner frequencies by the extreme raises the length sum from about three point zero zero eight to a ceiling of about four point zero six three.

00:06:05.654 --> 00:06:07.360
One loss depends on angle.

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:09.984
The other depends only on degree and radius.

00:06:10.284 --> 00:06:12.387
Which degrees reach the ceiling?

00:06:12.387 --> 00:06:13.966
Keep the radius at two.

00:06:13.966 --> 00:06:16.994
For degree zero, the only frequency is zero.

00:06:16.994 --> 00:06:20.364
The exact maximum and the ceiling both equal one.

00:06:20.364 --> 00:06:26.124
For degree one, both frequencies are already extreme, so both values are five quarters.

00:06:26.124 --> 00:06:29.878
Degree two introduces a middle term at frequency zero.

00:06:29.878 --> 00:06:33.141
The exact maximum is fifty-nine over thirty-two.

00:06:33.141 --> 00:06:35.978
The ceiling is seventeen eighths, which is larger.

00:06:35.978 --> 00:06:38.004
The same gap appeared at degree three.

00:06:38.004 --> 00:06:45.854
For every degree at least two and radius greater than one, a positive weight at an inner frequency makes the inequality strict.

00:06:46.760 --> 00:06:48.992
The Hardy norm measures a full turn.

00:06:48.992 --> 00:06:52.490
At one ellipse point, add the four complex arrows first.

00:06:52.490 --> 00:06:56.308
The squared length of their resultant is the energy at that angle.

00:06:56.308 --> 00:06:59.400
Move around the ellipse and watch that energy change.

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:02.045
The colour follows the height of the energy curve.

00:07:02.045 --> 00:07:04.818
Average those squared values over the full turn.

00:07:04.818 --> 00:07:07.378
The square root of that mean is the Hardy norm.

00:07:07.678 --> 00:07:09.760
Give equal angles equal weight.

00:07:09.760 --> 00:07:15.285
Divide the circle into equal angle steps, and mark the corresponding cells on the ellipse.

00:07:15.285 --> 00:07:16.927
Their arc lengths can differ.

00:07:16.927 --> 00:07:18.612
Read the energy in each cell.

00:07:18.612 --> 00:07:24.328
Its sample height times the angle step gives the area of a rectangle on the angular plot.

00:07:24.328 --> 00:07:28.360
As the steps get finer, the rectangle sum approaches the integral.

00:07:28.360 --> 00:07:31.453
Dividing by two pi gives the mean squared size.

00:07:31.753 --> 00:07:33.622
Watch a cross term rotate.

00:07:33.622 --> 00:07:37.654
Multiply the sum of the four modes by its complex conjugate.

00:07:37.654 --> 00:07:41.322
The grid shows each mode paired with each conjugate mode.

00:07:41.322 --> 00:07:46.399
A diagonal pair has frequency difference zero, so its contribution stays fixed.

00:07:46.399 --> 00:07:50.196
An off-diagonal pair has a nonzero integer difference.

00:07:50.196 --> 00:07:56.894
Its phase completes full turns as theta runs from zero to two pi, and its angular average is zero.

00:07:57.194 --> 00:07:58.957
Only squared lengths survive.

00:07:58.957 --> 00:08:03.308
After averaging the full grid, only four diagonal entries remain.

00:08:03.308 --> 00:08:05.398
Each is the square of one arrow length.

00:08:05.398 --> 00:08:07.339
This is Parseval's identity.

00:08:07.339 --> 00:08:13.631
Add the squares of five halves, three eighths, three over thirty-two, and five over one hundred twenty-eight.

00:08:13.631 --> 00:08:17.898
The exact mean squared size is about six point four zero one.

00:08:17.898 --> 00:08:21.866
Taking the square root gives a Hardy norm of about two point five three.

00:08:22.166 --> 00:08:24.547
Square the reciprocal radial factors.

00:08:24.547 --> 00:08:29.495
For the cubic pair, eight and one eighth become sixty-four and one over sixty-four.

00:08:29.495 --> 00:08:33.143
Multiply each by the square of its weight to get its energy.

00:08:33.143 --> 00:08:35.659
The doubled exponents belong to the radius.

00:08:35.659 --> 00:08:38.795
The original boundary waves still have three crests each.

00:08:39.095 --> 00:08:40.986
Drop one factor of each weight.

00:08:40.986 --> 00:08:47.300
Each colour starts with its exact Fourier energy, the squared weight times the doubled radial power.

00:08:47.300 --> 00:08:49.219
Keep its radial factor fixed.

00:08:49.219 --> 00:08:51.714
Replace the squared weight by the weight itself.

00:08:51.714 --> 00:08:56.002
Every weight lies between zero and one, so each bar can only grow.

00:08:56.002 --> 00:09:04.493
For example, the positive cubic term keeps its radial energy factor sixty-four while five sixteenths squared becomes five sixteenths.

00:09:04.793 --> 00:09:06.363
Pair the doubled powers.

00:09:06.363 --> 00:09:08.709
Read these bars on one energy scale.

00:09:08.709 --> 00:09:12.527
The exact squared norm is about six point four zero one.

00:09:12.527 --> 00:09:17.561
After dropping one weight from each term, the sum is about twenty point eight zero two.

00:09:17.561 --> 00:09:22.339
Pair the reciprocal radial factors with twice the logarithmic radius.

00:09:22.339 --> 00:09:24.557
Pairing keeps that intermediate total.

00:09:24.557 --> 00:09:32.279
Replacing all frequency magnitudes by the extreme gives the squared ceiling, about thirty-two point zero zero eight.

00:09:32.279 --> 00:09:33.857
Now take square roots.

00:09:33.857 --> 00:09:39.596
The Hardy norm is about two point five three, and its upper bound is about five point six five eight.

00:09:39.896 --> 00:09:41.466
Check the constant mode.

00:09:41.466 --> 00:09:44.986
At degree zero there is just one phase and it stays fixed.

00:09:44.986 --> 00:09:49.358
The output is always one, so the energy profile is flat at height one.

00:09:49.358 --> 00:09:52.280
Every equal-angle cell has the same energy.

00:09:52.280 --> 00:09:55.416
Its mean, its square root and both bounds are all one.

00:09:55.416 --> 00:09:59.660
As the radius tends to one, the ellipse collapses to the real interval.

00:09:59.660 --> 00:10:03.010
This is the limiting picture of the angular construction.

00:10:03.310 --> 00:10:05.904
Orthonormal scaling changes the size.

00:10:05.904 --> 00:10:09.936
For the real-line orthonormal mode, start with two n plus one.

00:10:09.936 --> 00:10:12.324
Divide by two and take the square root.

00:10:12.324 --> 00:10:16.484
Multiply the polynomial and both norm bounds by that factor.

00:10:16.484 --> 00:10:18.126
Squared energies take its square.

00:10:18.426 --> 00:10:20.274
Compare the two cubic graphs.

00:10:20.274 --> 00:10:25.265
At each angle, evaluate both polynomials at the same point on the same ellipse.

00:10:25.265 --> 00:10:28.251
The graphs show the real parts of those two values.

00:10:28.251 --> 00:10:32.560
For degree three, the scaling factor is the square root of seven halves.

00:10:32.560 --> 00:10:37.743
The heights are multiplied by that factor, and their zero crossings stay at the same angles.

00:10:37.743 --> 00:10:42.458
The full complex value and both of its norm bounds scale by the same factor.

00:10:42.758 --> 00:10:44.350
Return to the real line.

00:10:44.350 --> 00:10:51.325
Here x runs from minus one to one along the horizontal axis of an ordinary polynomial graph.

00:10:51.325 --> 00:10:56.039
In the previous plot, the horizontal coordinate was the angle around the ellipse.

00:10:56.039 --> 00:10:58.129
P zero is the constant one.

00:10:58.129 --> 00:11:01.158
Its graph stays at the same height across the interval.

00:11:01.158 --> 00:11:02.651
P one equals x.

00:11:02.651 --> 00:11:05.701
It rises along the diagonal through the origin.

00:11:05.701 --> 00:11:07.471
P two is the quadratic.

00:11:07.471 --> 00:11:11.950
Its graph dips to minus one half at the centre and reaches one at both ends.

00:11:11.950 --> 00:11:13.528
P three is the cubic.

00:11:13.528 --> 00:11:18.605
It crosses the horizontal axis three times and has two interior turning points.

00:11:18.605 --> 00:11:21.527
Compare P three with its orthonormal version.

00:11:21.527 --> 00:11:25.516
Multiply the height at each x by the square root of seven halves.

00:11:25.516 --> 00:11:32.300
The zeros and turning points keep their horizontal positions, while the extreme heights are scaled by that same factor.

